"""Interactive, browser-local calculation models with visible assumptions."""

def field(ident,label,value,minimum=0,maximum=None,step=1):
    return f'<label for="{ident}">{label}<input type="number" id="{ident}" value="{value}" min="{minimum}"'+(f' max="{maximum}"' if maximum is not None else '')+f' step="{step}"></label>'

def lab(ident,title,controls,assumption):
    return f'<section class="lab extra-lab" id="lab-{ident}" data-model="{ident}"><span class="eyebrow">THE WORKBENCH · TRY CHANGING THE INPUTS</span><h3>{title}</h3><div class="lab-controls">{controls}</div><div class="lab-output" id="model-{ident}-result" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="assumption">{assumption}</p><noscript><p>The lesson and figures explain the example without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to change the inputs here.</p></noscript></section>'

BENCHES={
2:lab('capacity','Estimate capacity for the social application',field('model-users','Daily active users',10000000)+field('model-requests','API requests per user/day',50)+field('model-peak','Peak multiplier',5,1,100,.5)+field('model-rps','Requests/s per instance (assumed)',600,1)+field('model-utilization','Target utilization fraction',.6,.01,.99,.01),'Start with the stated daily workload, then change one input. Each user views ten 200 kB images and writes one 2 kB post per day. GB and TB use decimal units. The instance capacity is an assumption; database capacity and capacity after a larger outage need their own checks.'),
4:lab('queue','What happens when jobs arrive faster than they finish?',field('model-arrivals','Jobs arriving each second',150)+field('model-service','Jobs workers finish each second',100)+field('model-duration','Seconds to simulate',60)+field('model-buffer','Maximum waiting jobs',100),'Start with 150 arrivals and 100 completions each second. The extra jobs must wait or be rejected. Increase the queue limit: does it make workers finish faster, or only let more jobs wait? This model starts empty and treats jobs as a steady flow; it does not simulate individual threads or storage.'),
10:lab('ticket','Let two callers claim the last seat','<label for="model-ticket-policy">Claim policy<select id="model-ticket-policy"><option value="naive">Read then unprotected write</option><option value="conditional">Atomic conditional claim</option></select></label>','Compare the two policies using the same order of steps. Does either caller learn that the seat has already been taken? This display replays a fixed example. The downloadable SQLite example separately tests the atomic conditional update.'),
11:lab('lru','Follow which cache entry is used least recently','<label for="model-cache-access">Access sequence, comma separated<input id="model-cache-access" value="A,B,C,A,D" maxlength="200"></label>'+field('model-cache-capacity','Cache entries',3,1,20),'The cache holds a fixed number of entries. Each access loads a missing key or moves an existing key to the most recently used end. Real caches may use approximate eviction, byte limits, and expiry. Choosing an entry to evict does not tell us whether its value is fresh.'),
12:lab('hash','Compare owners before and after adding D',field('model-hash-key','Selected integer key',190,0,1000000),'Change the key and compare its owner before and after D joins. The same key always receives the same scores in this example. Choosing a new owner does not copy data, agree on membership, or spread the traffic of a single popular key.'),
13:lab('pipeline','Should this worker be allowed to finish?','<label for="model-pipeline-scenario">Attempt scenario<select id="model-pipeline-scenario"><option value="stale">Epoch 7 completes after epoch 8 owns run</option><option value="wrong">Current epoch uses old input version F1</option><option value="valid">Current epoch 8 uses source F2</option><option value="deleted">Document was deleted before completion</option></select></label>','Choose an attempt and check its ownership epoch, input version, and document state. A real database must check these conditions and save the result atomically. This step does not include an external model call or an object-store write.'),
14:lab('quorum','When must a read set meet a write set?',field('model-quorum-n','Replica count N',3,1,9)+field('model-quorum-r','Read set size R',2,1,9)+field('model-quorum-w','Write set size W',2,1,9),'Keep the same N replicas, then vary the number read and written. The calculation shows whether the sets must share a member. To decide which value a read can return, we also need rules for versions, concurrent writes, persistence, repair, and membership changes. Intersection alone does not prove linearizability.'),
21:lab('cost','Calculate the cost of one successful outcome',field('model-outcomes','Successful outcomes/month',150000,1),'These invented monthly costs are 371, 551, and 1,402 units. Change the number of successful outcomes to see the cost per outcome. Before comparing the options, test whether each can meet the required capacity, latency, availability, and correctness. These figures are not provider prices.')}
BUCKET=lab('bucket','Use a burst of tokens, then let the bucket refill','<div class="lab-actions"><button type="button" id="model-bucket-spend">Spend one token</button><button type="button" id="model-bucket-advance">Advance 3 seconds</button><button type="button" id="model-bucket-reset">Reset</button></div>','The bucket holds at most ten tokens and gains two per second. Spend tokens, then advance the simulated clock. One browser owns this bucket. A shared implementation must update the balance atomically; several independent owners need a protocol to enforce a global limit.')
